cy's Q1 2026\npress release, 2026-06-03; FRED; Fed H.15) + analysis.\n\nHeadline conclusion: EXPECTED VALUE $24.75/share vs price $22.82\n(+8.5%). No margin of safety. The market has already repriced this\nstock to near its probability-weighted fair value after a 5-quarter\nbeat streak. At $22.82 you are paying for the base case and getting\nthe bear case for free in the wrong direction \u2014 the risk-reward is\nasymmetric TO THE DOWNSIDE at this price. Enter only on a pullback\nbelow $19.50. Action: HOLD / NO NEW BUY.\n\nData-map time pointers (critical):\n- Pack \"FY2026\" = fiscal year ended 2026-01-31 = company \"Fiscal\n  2025\". Confirmed against company release: net sales $21.8B,\n  GAAP EPS $2.32 [Actual \u2014 company EX-99.1].\n- Pack TTM through 2026-05-02 = quarters Q2'26\u2013Q1'27 (company\n  naming) = $21.847B revenue / $667M NI / FCF $1.425B [Filed].\n- Pack quarter \"2026-01-31\" ($7.639B, $507M) = company Q4 fiscal\n  2025, matches company release Q4 net sales $7.6B, NI $507M.\n- Pack FY2024 net income ($45M) CONFLICTS with company release GAAP\n  FY2024 net income $582M / adjusted $745M. Pack figure treated as\n  mis-mapped; company release used for that year. Flagged in Layer 8.\n- Company FY2026 guidance (ending Jan-2027), raised 2026-06-03:\n  net sales $21.5\u201321.75B, comp +0.5% to +1.2%, adj EBITDA margin\n  7.7\u20137.9%, adj diluted EPS $2.00\u20132.20 [Actual].\n\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n1-1 Macro variables (T0 = Aug 2026):\n- 10Y UST yield: 4.70% (2026-08-21, FRED DGS10) [Actual]; 4.74% on\n  2026-08-20; up from 4.30% one year ago. Direction: rising.\n- Real rate: 10Y TIPS \u2248 +1.9% [Estimate \u2014 computed, not retrieved].\n- Fed funds: 3.50\u20133.75%, unchanged for 5 consecutive meetings,\n  July 2026 (TradingEconomics; Fed calendar) [Actual]. Bias:\n  neutral-to-hawkish; ~1-in-3 odds of a HIKE priced (Octagon,\n  July 2026) [Estimate]. Stance: TIGHTENING, not easing.\n- DXY: level and trend NOT RETRIEVED (research budget exhausted)\n  [Unconfirmed].\n- Credit spreads: not retrieved [Unconfirmed]; note Macy's has no\n  material maturities until 2030 [Actual \u2014 company Q1 2026 release].\n- Economic cycle: late-cycle consumer (retail sales holding;\n  luxury bifurcation visible in Macy's own Blooie's +10.2% comp vs\n  Macy's nameplate +1.6%).\n- Inflation: persistent enough to keep the Fed on hold through\n  mid-2026 [Estimate].\n\n1-2 Regime probability distribution:\n- Goldilocks:                                15%\n- Reflation (high rates + growth):           35%   \u2190 modal\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth):     30%\n- Recession (disinflation + contraction):    20%\n(Total = 100%. These are my regime probabilities for the next 12\nmonths, derived from the Fed's hawkish hold, the flat/declining\nretail sales tape, and the elevated 10Y.)\n\n1-3 Sector macro sensitivity (M, \u22123 to +3):\n- Interest-rate sensitivity:   \u22122  (discretionary credit; store-card\n                                   receivables)\n- Dollar sensitivity:          \u22121  (importer; tariffs partially\n                                   offset by USD strength)\n- Liquidity sensitivity:       \u22122  (high-ticket discretionary)\n- Economic-cycle sensitivity:  \u22123  (department stores are max-beta\n                                   consumer cyclicals)\n- Commodity sensitivity:       \u22121  (freight, cotton, energy)\n\n1-4 Macro alignment: \u2014 weighted average of regime matrix is a net\nHEADWIND (Reflation + Stagflation = 65% probability, both bad for a\nhigh-ticket discretionary retailer with tariff exposure).\nConsequence: Conviction \u22120.5 step; strengthen split-entry strategy;\nno valuation penalty applied to intrinsic value.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE-RATE CHECK\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n1.5-1 Base rates (reference set, v2.0 framework):\n- Probability a mature department-store retailer with this margin\n  profile survives as a going concern 5 years: ~60\u201370% (industry\n  failure base rate elevated: Sears, JCPenney, Saks bankruptcy\n  Jan-2026 [Actual, Bloomberg] all failed/restructured).\n- Probability a sub-6% operating-margin retailer reaches 8%+ OI\n  within 5 years: ~15\u201325% (retail turnaround base rate).\n- Probability a company in this archetype (mature, market-share\n  losing, high fixed-cost) generates positive FCF over a decade:\n  ~65% (cash generation is real until the very end).\n- Probability of M&A/private-equity takeout with ROIC > WACC for a\n  ~$6.9B retail REIT-plus-stores play: ~25\u201335%.\n- Target-price-anchored consensus success rate: 47% of price targets\n  are met in 12 months (industry average; my reference set).\n\n1.5-2 Deviation of my thesis from base rate: My base case (still a\ngoing concern with declining revenue but stable-to-rising margins)\nis LOWER than the base rate for survival and HIGHER than the base\nrate for margin expansion. I justify the margin-expansion deviation\nby three company-specific facts: (1) 150 underproductive stores are\nclosing and their fixed costs exit with them \u2014 same revenue, lower\ncost base; (2) Reimagine 200 stores are comping +2.4% vs the name-\nplate +1.6% [Actual \u2014 Q1 2026 release]; (3) SG&A is declining in\nabsolute dollars while revenue is roughly flat (pack FY2026 SG&A\n$8.24B vs FY2024 $8.46B) [Filed].\n\n1.5-3 Base-rate shock check: My bull-case margin (8.5% adj EBITDA\nfor 10 years) is not 1.5\u00d7 the framework's success base rate \u2014\nPASS. My base case does not imply FY2030 revenue growth above the\nindustry real rate \u2014 PASS. No red flag triggered.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n2-1 Business understanding (3 sentences):\nMacy's Inc. runs three retail nameplates \u2014 Macy's (~350 go-forward\ndepartment stores + digital), Bloomingdale's (modern luxury, stores +\ndigital), and Bluemercury (beauty, ~160 locations) \u2014 selling\napparel, accessories, beauty and home. It monetizes the traffic two\nadditional ways: a branded credit-card portfolio (FY2025 credit-card\nnet revenues $669M [Actual \u2014 company EX-99.1]) and Macy's Media\nNetwork ad sales ($188M). FY2025 (ending Jan-2026) net sales were\n$21.8B with go-forward comps +1.7% and company-defined FCF $0.8B.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\n2-2 Moat assessment (0\u20133 each):\n- Intangible assets (brand):      2  \u2014 Macy's is the largest US\n  department store by share (~40%, csimarket [Estimate]); brand\n  carries real value; Herald Square is iconic.\n- Switching costs:                1  \u2014 loyalty program exists; low.\n- Network effects:                0  \u2014 none.\n- Cost advantage:                 1  \u2014 largest scale in the channel\n  but structurally high-cost vs off-price/online.\n- Economies of scale:             2  \u2014 real in private brands,\n  marketing, media network; overwhelmed by channel decline.\n- Regulatory barriers:            0  \u2014 none.\n- Data moat:                      2  \u2014 25M+ loyal customers, credit\n  card spend data, Media Network targeting.\nTOTAL: 8/21 \u2192 NARROW MOAT.\nMoat direction: NARROWING. 2015 operating income $2.80B \u2192 FY2026\n$1.03B. Market share in total US retail is eroding. However, the\nrate of narrowing slowed in the last two fiscal years (OI $0.30B \u2192\n$0.91B \u2192 $1.03B) and Reimagine stores + Bloomingdale's are widening\nwithin the channel.\n\n2-3 Lynch classification: CYCLICAL with a secular decline overlay \u2014\nbut the useful frame is the classic asset-play/turnaround hybrid:\nthe operating business earns 4.7% OI and